# Line endings, pinned at the repository root.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# Without an explicit attribute, the line endings in a fresh checkout depend on
# whatever `core.autocrlf` the cloning machine happens to have. Most of this
# repository is developed on Windows and deployed to Ubuntu, which is exactly
# the combination where that bites:
#
#   * A single commit made with CRLF in scripts/deploy.sh turns every line of
#     the script into `<command>\r` on the server. The shell reports
#     `\r: command not found` or, worse, silently passes a trailing carriage
#     return as part of a path or a variable — `[ "$code" = "200" ]` stops
#     matching, `sudo install ... "$FILE\r"` fails, and the deploy dies halfway.
#   * nginx and systemd both accept CRLF in their config files, but a stray \r
#     ends up INSIDE directive values: `add_header ... "no-store\r"`,
#     `Environment="JWT_SECRET=secret\r"`. Nothing fails loudly; the value is
#     just wrong.
#
# So: everything that is executed or parsed on the Linux host is pinned to LF
# in the working tree, not just in the repository.
#
# launcher/.gitattributes deliberately overrides this for *.cs and *.xaml
# (CRLF — .editorconfig requires it and `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` is
# a blocking CI step). The more specific file wins, so the two do not conflict.

# Default: let git decide what is text, and normalise it to LF in the repo.
* text=auto

# --- Executed or parsed on the Linux host: LF is load-bearing ---
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.timer     text eol=lf
*.socket    text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf
*.mk        text eol=lf

# Go sources and module files: gofmt and the toolchain assume LF.
*.go        text eol=lf
go.mod      text eol=lf
go.sum      text eol=lf

# Web assets served from the Linux host.
*.html      text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# --- Windows-side scripts: CRLF, so they open correctly in Windows editors ---
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.psm1      text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.nsi       text eol=crlf

# --- Binary: never touch, never diff as text ---
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.webp      binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
*.7z        binary
*.exe       binary
*.dll       binary
*.pdb       binary
*.ttf       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
